t of time  had been spent talking about the  framework and what was going to happen  what we found over the past five years  is that many many organizations that  people have actually bought into this as  their frameworks most corporations have  SDG frameworks for looking at their  corporate social responsibility  universities teach courses on it and  have labs about it  foundations give money to the SDGs  financial institutions set up investment  funds around the sustainable development  goals many cities have their own plans  every single country in the world has  bought into it one way or another  what are the reasons if that's happened  is that they've been allowed to select  which goals and targets they're going to  go after and what that means is that  each country has a customized plan that  meets their needs so everything from the  United States and the UK to the poorest  and least developed country in the world  is involved with this China Russia  everybody so it's been exciting because  from having nothing  we actually have too much data now so a  lot of our work has been about the too  much data so we'll talk a little bit  about that and how we've we've we've  pivoted a few times in terms of what  we've been doing for a couple of  different reasons  so the SDG counting project when we  started out the idea was been had been  in Swaziland working in health  development on a mission and he was the  guy who did most of the metrics and  reporting and years ago before the SDG  started better than I started talking  about he would enter his data by being  with the people in the field who were  doing the health work  and I had another friend named Chris  Collins who was working at the UN and he  was the guy who got all the role to  update at the very top and we were all  fascinated about how much the data and  the conclusions changed from where Ben  was entering it at the very beginning to  where Chris was working with the  consolidators at the very top and what  we noticed is that in a roll up of  information you you lose things you gain  things you average things out so the the  15 year goal of this project is is what  I'd like to say you know write wants  read many so the data is captured as  close to the ground as possible and  rolls all the way up without being  changed its consolidated and things like  that so that you are having the same  data picture of the world so that when  people are doing metrics they can all be  looking at the same data again  aspirational so Eric our project is just  as aspirational as the SVG goals are but  what the heck you have 15 years we  decided that Wolfram was perfect for  this because it's iterative you can add  to it over time new things are coming  out all the time as the technology  changes you don't lose your coding we  thought we wouldn't have to switch and  it was relatively easy to use at the  very beginning I was the one who was  doing the coding and I'm not a coder  since over the past five years I've  started working with with others who  were better coders than me  much better corners than me and we've  been looking at how to integrate and now  how to look at all the different data  sources and begin to understand how they  relate to each other so we've been  looking Ben mentioned earlier and Benji  mentioned that we've been looking at SDG  14 which is about life under the sea  because not too many people were looking  at it we had some other reasons so our  revolution has been when I go back to  the presentation that I gave here five  years ago  we've accomplished a whole lot but we  have an accomplishment I thought we  would but we'll keep on track of their  this the other thing we did was  selecting projects based on where there  were niches for us what we at the  beginning we didn't know what was going  to happen in terms of counting so what  we thought was going to happen was that  we would be consolidating the  information that came into the UN what  we found is there's plenty of people  doing that and bet we'll be talking a  little bit more about that but what we  found really interesting is that where  the action is is actually around  startups around investments and around  what's going on in the startup and and  innovation ecosystems so we've been  focusing on that more over the past  several years so our current work our  badge is some of our badges at least  they starting up good so we have two  different organizations one called  starting up good which is working with  startups to help them understand how to  position themselves better for getting  investment and how to use the  sustainable development goals as a way  to position themselves we do all of our  workers pro bono we have clients pro  bono clients we went to New York Tech  Day last year and found six startups  that were already working in so doing  things with social impact energy  management using music for PTSD looking  at journalism in a more fair way looking  at what's going on with SDG 14 and some  others and we brought them on with us  because we wanted to be working in the  real world not just with data in our  last presentation we talked a little  about Twitter and there's a lot more but  what we found is that it's very  important to be looking at social media  and how you're positioning yourself in  order to be attractive to impact  investors you want to be where the  impact investors are and Sgt counting  while we talk  about a little more later today and  impact investing is sort of the fulcrum  of where we are on a lot of our  different projects so I wanted to take  you through a couple of small case  studies of things that we've been doing  the first one is looking at building  computational knowledge on SGG 14 which  is life under water about conserving and  sustainably using the oceans seas and  marine resources for sustainable  development and here what we wanted to  do is figure out how to get as much  comprehensive information as possible on  one of the SDGs and to amplify the  ability of the subject matter expert in  the BlueTEC economy in order to  influence the field so one of the people  we've been working with a guy named  chris is an expert in bringing people  together a convener and he knows a whole  lot of people who were working in  BlueTEC and on the SDGs and what we  found is that it's very important to  combine human intelligence with the  kinds of things that Wolfram can do for  you  so Canet liaison with people with the  different pro bono clients spend a lot  of time talking with chris and doing you  could formally call it knowledge  engineering but what she was actually  doing was sort of picking his brain to  try to understand all the people he's  met and all the things that he does and  putting it into a form that could be  used as data for future projects and so  a lot of time had been spent doing that  and she used LinkedIn connections  Twitter personal conversations it went  into a data set we amplified that by  using wolfram web search to look at the  ecosystem we used web image search  Twitter Wikipedia data and we use text  cases to refine the data and what we  found in all of our work is that there  are so many different places where small  pieces of Wolfram code can be really  helpful to moving ahead the project we  cross-referenced all of our web search  findings to identify knowledge gaps and  mixed network connections in the  influenced person influencers personal  map and we're working now with Chris to  use the information so that he can go  out and make connections with people  make connections with the right people  be attending the right events have his  social media presence be somebody be  some way that sees him as a convener and  we ultimately are hoping to use SDG 14  and the work that we've done with Chris  to as a model for all 17 of the SDGs and  to publish online a compendium of where  all the information is where you can  find it and what you can do with it I 